            contentEn: 'There is something funny about what <strong>happened. Wundt\'s method was forgotten. His idea won.</strong></p><p>Introspection proved problematic: different people report different things about the same stimulus, the reports cannot be verified from outside, the training takes years and still does not guarantee accuracy. <strong>By the early 20th century, psychology had abandoned introspection almost entirely.</strong></p><p>But Wundt\'s laboratory gave birth to something deeper: the idea that <strong>the mind is an object of scientific inquiry. In</strong> the 50 years after Leipzig, hundreds of psychology laboratories sprang up around the world. Wundt\'s students founded the first departments in America, in Britain, in Russia.</p><p><strong>Every modern psychologist - from the psychoanalyst in Manhattan to the brain researcher in California - owes Wundt the ground on which he stands.</strong></p><p>And this also opens the great debate of the chapter. If the mind is a scientific object - how do we study it? Wundt said: through a person\'s report on their own consciousness. James will propose something else. Freud will propose something else entirely. Skinner will argue that consciousness must not be studied at all. <strong>Every one of them - including those who will attack Wundt sharply - takes for granted that this is a field for research.</strong></p><p>And in this, Wundt changed the world.',
