            contentEn: 'Cambridge, Massachusetts, late 19th century. While Wundt in Leipzig is trying to break consciousness down into basic components, an American philosopher-psychologist named William James looks at that same project and says: this <strong>is a fundamental mistake.</strong></p><p>James was not a laboratory man. He was a fine teacher, a stormy intellectual, a man who himself suffered mental crises and understood that the human mind is not atoms. <strong>Consciousness, he claimed, is not a sequence of discrete moments. It is a river.</strong></p><p>Think about the minute passing for you right now. Is it composed of separate "moments of consciousness"? Or does it flow, wind, shift, one thought flowing into another, an emotion interposing, a memory rising, a glance out the window diverting <strong>attention? James argued that the second possibility is</strong> the correct one. And that makes Wundt\'s "chemistry of consciousness" impossible.</p><p><strong>There are no atoms of consciousness. There is flow.</strong>',
