            explanationEn: 'Until Freud, the <strong>West took pride in rationality. Descartes: "I think, therefore I am." The Enlightenment proposed a person with self-control, grounded in reason.</strong> Freud reversed the picture: most of what we do is not rational, and not conscious. Patterns from childhood, repressed desires, uncontrollable fears, these are what run our lives, not the "conscious self." This was a threatening idea, but also a liberating one.',
